{"event": "span_end", "trace_id": "trace_f33bb4f266734c4f955c5ed7490ac4cc", "span_id": "span_e63eedee314f4ae39c055701", "parent_id": "span_252210af003341dbaab3a3b4", "type": "Generation", "depth": 3, "duration": 4912.69, "detail": "\u2192 output: [{'content': None, 'refusal': None, 'role': 'assistant', 'annotations': None, 'audio': None, 'function_call': None, 'tool_calls': [{'id': 'call_556783', 'function': {'arguments': '{\"query\":\"Nigerian defence companies manufacturing armoured vehicles 2025 2026\"}', 'name': 'web_search'}, 'type': 'function', 'extra_content': {'google': {'thought_signature': 'EnEKbwERTTIPY23Rt3WtQH+mya3k212JG9zo6UV3q21n4N/ocGV3QJFt/wByznAXblEU+doGRkFPnwCcnWCaz+upxMQETs9NYYvfl9j8xh/EAbgRVctFS041nuFFdZu7NpkX+dSsGI5axSNCIPb395qDGA=='}}}]}]", "logged_at": "2026-08-13T10:37:50.757735+00:00"}
{"event": "span_start", "trace_id": "trace_f33bb4f266734c4f955c5ed7490ac4cc", "span_id": "span_dc8b9a39fa644737b3f01401", "parent_id": "span_252210af003341dbaab3a3b4", "type": "Function", "depth": 3, "logged_at": "2026-08-13T10:37:50.757735+00:00"}
{"event": "span_end", "trace_id": "trace_f33bb4f266734c4f955c5ed7490ac4cc", "span_id": "span_89a6fe8cb67143e88893c588", "parent_id": "span_efec2744d7714586bf8800a3", "type": "Function", "depth": 3, "duration": 4314.76, "detail": "\u2192 output: Summary:\nThe Defence Industries Corporation of Nigeria (DICON) continues to produce licensed foreign designs, but shows increasing capability in local manufacturing, with a focus on armored vehicles for counter-insurgency missions. DICON aims to become a key provider of indigenous armored solutions. The outlook for 2026 suggests continued reliance on licensed production.\n\nSources:\n\n\n        1.\n        Title: Nigeria\u2019s evolving defence industry - Global Defence Technology | Issue 158 | Summer 2026\n        URL: https://defence.nridigital.com/global_defence_technology_sum26/nigeria_s_evolving_defence_industry\n        Content:\n        Analyst Comment\n\n# Nigeria\u2019s evolving defence industry\n\n### Nigeria\u2019s defence industry is evolving, but reliance on license builds will continue. \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200bCallum Kaye reports.\n\nPowered by\n\nMain video supplied by werbeantrieb/Creatas Video+ / Getty Images Plus via Getty Images\n\nIn March 2026, 15 Vanquisher light armoured personnel carriers, manufactured by the Defense Industries Corporation of Nigeria (DICON) in collaboration with Vanquish Industries Limited, were delivered to the Nigerian Army.\n\nBeing a locally manufactured vehicle, the Vanquisher is an indication of the Nigerian defence industry\u2019s growing self-sufficiency. [...] The primary player in Nigeria\u2019s defence industry is state-owned DICON, itself run by the Nigerian Armed Forces. Established in 1964, DICON has typically manufactured licensed copies of foreign designs, with a focus on small arms-light weapons (SALW) and body armour. Prominent examples include the NR1, a licensed FN FAL, and the DG-103, a licensed Kalashnikov AK-103 manufactured by the DICON-D7G joint venture.\n\nIn recent years, Nigeria\u2019s defence industry capability has expanded to encompass mine-resistant ambush-protected (MRAP) vehicles, to meet the Nigerian Army\u2019s demand for such vehicles to conduct counter-insurgency (COIN) missions against groups such as Boko Haram. The most common of these is DICON\u2019s Ezugwu, whi", "logged_at": "2026-08-13T10:37:52.499175+00:00"}
{"event": "span_end", "trace_id": "trace_f33bb4f266734c4f955c5ed7490ac4cc", "span_id": "span_efec2744d7714586bf8800a3", "parent_id": "span_53b9434140dd4c14988352b9", "type": "Turn", "depth": 2, "duration": 6658.4, "detail": "", "logged_at": "2026-08-13T10:37:52.500176+00:00"}
